EXAMPLE (topic: Magnets)
### Beginner (student-facing)
Magnets pull on some metals. Try it: touch a magnet to a paper clip. Does it stick? Circle: YES / NO. Hint: look at the picture of the paper clip.
### Intermediate (student-facing)
Magnets attract objects made of iron, nickel or cobalt. Predict which 4 classroom objects a magnet will pick up. Test them. Explain one result in a full sentence.
### Advanced / Challenge (student-facing)
Explain why a magnet picks up a steel nail but not an aluminium can. Design a fair test for 5 materials and say what you will keep the same.
NOW WRITE THIS FOR THE LESSON ABOVE, using these exact headings:

## D. Differentiated Learning Versions
### 1. Beginner (short explanations, familiar words, worked example, visual supports, hints, small steps, frequent checks for understanding)
### 2. Intermediate (grade-level explanation, independent practice, mix of recall/comprehension/application, explain reasoning)
### 3. Advanced / Challenge (problem-solving, analysis, investigation, extension; compare, justify, predict or design)

