Write section E. Use the two examples below to see how the SAME concept is adapted for each level, including the dyslexia-friendly version.

EXAMPLE 1 (topic: Magnets)
Beginner: Magnets pull on some metals. Touch a magnet to a paper clip. Does it stick?
Intermediate: Magnets attract iron, nickel and cobalt. Predict which objects a magnet picks up, then test.
Advanced: Explain why a magnet picks up a steel nail but not an aluminium can.
Dyslexia-friendly: Key words: **magnet**, **attract**. Word bank: magnet | metal | stick. Steps: 1. Hold the magnet near a paper clip. 2. Does it stick? 3. Say your answer out loud or draw it. Sentence starter: "The magnet sticks to ___."

EXAMPLE 2 (topic: Plants need light)
Beginner: Plants need light to grow. A plant in a dark box gets weak.
Intermediate: Plants use light to make food. Predict what happens to a plant kept in the dark for one week.
Advanced: Design an experiment to test how different amounts of light change plant growth.
Dyslexia-friendly: Key words: **light**, **grow**. Word bank: plant | light | dark. Steps: 1. Look at plant A (light). 2. Look at plant B (dark). 3. Draw both plants. Sentence starter: "The plant in the dark looks ___."

NOW WRITE THIS FOR THE LESSON ABOVE, using these exact headings:

## E. Dyslexia-Friendly Learning Version
### Vocabulary Preview and Word Bank (new words in small groups)
### Short Explanation (concise sentences, short sections with descriptive headings, key terms in bold, not colour alone)
### Guided Example (before independent work)
### Student Tasks (numbered sequential steps, short task sections, sentence starters)
### Ways to Show Understanding (oral answers, diagrams, sorting, practical demonstration)
### Adaptations Used (extra processing time, text-to-speech or audio, no penalty for spelling or decoding errors, clear layout, sans-serif font in the final document, and the purpose of each)

Treat the strategies as adaptable options, not as the needs of every student with dyslexia. Keep the same scientific content and learning expectations as the other versions.

